<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Melrose Abbey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1136</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Melrose Abbey is a ruined Cistercian abbey in the Scottish Borders, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> I in 1136, the first Cistercian house in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. It grew rich from sheep farming and the wool trade, and the heart of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-the-bruce">Robert the Bruce</a> is believed to be buried there. Repeatedly damaged by English armies, it was largely rebuilt in the late fourteenth century and declined after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/melrose-abbey.jpg" alt="Melrose Abbey" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gitta Zahn (@gittaz on Flickr) · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ruins_of_St_Mary%27s_Abbey,_Melrose_(Scotland%27s_first_Cistercian_monastery,_1136),_showing_late_14th-century_Gothic_architecture_in_rose-colored_sandstone_with_medieval_cemetery,_burial_place_of_Robert_the_Bruce%27s_heart,_Scottish_Borders.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>An earlier monastery, Old Melrose, stood about 2.5 miles east in a bend of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-tweed">River Tweed</a>, founded by Saint Aidan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne">Lindisfarne</a> shortly before his death in 651. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-cuthbert">Saint Cuthbert</a> trained there and was its prior from 662 before moving to Lindisfarne, and Kenneth I of Scotland raided the site in 839. David I wanted the new Cistercian house on the same spot, but the monks judged the land too poor for farming and chose the present site. Its first community came from Rievaulx Abbey, and its church was dedicated to the Virgin Mary on 28 July 1146.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Melrose became the mother church of the order in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, with 100 monks, wide lands from its founder and fishing rights on the Tweed. Its granges in the Lammermuirs led to grazing disputes with the tenants of the Earl of Dunbar and with the Morville lords of Lauderdale. Waltheof, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>'s stepson and abbot from 1148 to 1159, gave it a name for holiness and learning, and his tomb drew pilgrims. The Chronicle of Melrose holds one of the earliest accounts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magna-carta">Magna Carta</a>.</p><p>Lying on a main road south from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>, the abbey was largely destroyed by the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-ii-of-england">Edward II of England</a> in 1322 and rebuilt on the orders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-the-bruce">Robert the Bruce</a>, with Sir James Douglas overseeing its finances, then burned by the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-ii-of-england">Richard II of England</a> in 1385 and rebuilt once more. English armies damaged it again in 1544, during the effort to force the marriage of the infant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-queen-of-scots">Mary, Queen of Scots</a>, to Henry VIII's son, and it was never fully repaired. From 1541 commendators held the abbacy, and the last monk died in 1590.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a>'s troops later bombarded the walls, and part of the church served as the parish church from 1618 to 1810. Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-scott">Walter Scott</a>, as Sheriff-Depute of Roxburghshire, supervised repairs paid for by the Duke of Buccleuch, who gave the ruins to the state in 1918. The abbey keeps carved saints, dragons and gargoyles and is the only Scottish abbey with some original floor tiles. A lead casket holding a heart, found under the Chapter House in 1921 and presumed to be the Bruce's, was reburied there on 22 June 1998. Alexander II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> is among those buried at Melrose.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Melrose Abbey” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The church was rich, with great reformed abbeys such as <b>Melrose</b>, Kelso and Holyrood, and the founding of universities at St Andrews (1413), Glasgow (1451) and Aberdeen (1495) gave Scotland three universities when England still had two.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
